Thanks for your valuable suggestion. 
First, on ImageNet, we have tested our method MTA on three adversarially trained models.
 The three models are all publicly available on Github and have been adopted as target models by lots of previous transfer attack papers.

Second, following your valuable suggestion, we conduct another experiment on Cifar-10 to evaluate our method. In this experiment, we retrain the three models Res-18$\_{adv}$, Res-34$\_{adv}$, and SeRes-50$\_{adv}$ with $\epsilon$ = 15, which is consistent with $\epsilon$ used in inference. Specifically, to obtain XXX$\_{adv}$, we first use FGSM and the normally trained XXX model to generate adversarial examples for all training images, with $\epsilon$ = 15. Then we train XXX$\_{adv}$ on all the generated adversarial examples and the clean examples without data augmentation. The three retrained models Res-18$\_{adv}$, Res-34$\_{adv}$, SeRes-50$\_{adv}$ obtain approximately 78.3\%, 79.6\%, and 75.5\% accuracies on Cifar-10.

Finally, the transfer attack success rates on the three retrained models Res-18$\_{adv}$, Res-34$\_{adv}$, SeRes-50$\_{adv}$ are:  
The success rates of PGD are 17.2%, 18.3%, and 21.5%;  
The success rates of MI are 25.7%, 27.3%, and 31.8%;  
The success rates of TI are 27.1%, 25.9%, and 39.9%;  
The success rates of DI are 18.6%, 19.5%, and 24.0%;  
The success rates of TI-DIM are 24.9%, 22.3%, and 38.3%;  
The success rates of MTA are **31.9%**, **33.7%**, and **41.5%**.  
We will include the results of this experiment in our revision. This experiment further demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed MTA.

We did not try to use better-performing source models to attack worse target models. The reason why we chose the eight source models and eight target models is that we want to ensure mismatches between the source and target models so transfer attacks become nontrivial. If we use improper models as target models, the transfer attack performances are usually saturated. For instance, when attacking VGG16 and DenseNet121, MTA can achieve saturated performances of 99.9%, and some other baselines such as M-PGD can also achieve saturated performances around 99.0%.
When attacking VGG16, DenseNet121, and Inception-V3,  
The success rates of PGD are 78.1%, 78.2%, and 73.4%;  
The success rates of MI are 99.0%, 99.2%, and 96.1%;  
The success rates of TI are 75.1%, 72.3%, and 68.5%;  
The success rates of DI are 95.7%, 96.2%, and 85.4%;  
The success rates of MI-DI are 98.9%, 98.6%, and 94.9%;  
The success rates of MTA are **99.9%, 99.9%, and 99.7%;**  
It is clear that 1) when attacking these three target models, the experimental performances of MTA are easier to be saturated (near 100%); and 2) MTA outperforms other methods with big margins. These experiments further demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed MTA.

We also tried another experiment on Cifar-10 to address your concern.
In this experiment, we use MobileNet-V3, ShuffleNet-V1, ShuffleNet-V2, SqueezeNet-A, and SqueezeNet-B as the source models, and use ResNet-10, ResNet-18, ResNet-34, SeResNet-14, SeResNet-26, SeResNet-50, MobileNet-V1, MobileNet-V2 as the target models.
Obviously, the testing accuracies of source models are lower than those of the target models in this experiment.
The transfer attack success rates on ResNet-10, ResNet-18, ResNet-34, SeResNet-14, SeResNet-26, SeResNet-50, MobileNet-V1, MobileNet-V2 are:  
The success rates of PGD are 46.9%, 42.5%, 50.1%, 49.6%, 50.2%, 45.9%, 47.9%, and 54.5%;  
The success rates of MI are 89.5%, 86.1%, 90.7%, 88.3%, 91.0%, 89.1%, 86.6%, and 88.8%;  
The success rates of TI are 48.1%, 39.2%, 49.9%, 53.8%, 55.6%, 47.8%, 63.9%, and 60.8%;  
The success rates of DI are 65.2%, 56.9%, 69.6%, 70.2%, 71.5%, 65.7%, 69.5%, and 71.2%;    
The success rates of MTA are **96.7%, 94.6%, 98.3%, 98.7%, 98.8%, 97.5%, 96.7%, and 98.7%;**  
It is clear that **either using better performance source models to attack worse target models or using worse performance source models to attack better target models, the proposed MTA clearly outperforms the baselines.**
We hope that our responses have addressed your concerns. If you have any other comments, we are glad to address them in the rebuttal window. Thanks.
